1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
Ask an A.I. one simple question.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:04,610
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,400
Before it reads your question,

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,560
the machine chops it into pieces.

5
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,750
In English... just eight.

6
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:12,560
Now ask it in Hindi.

7
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:13,450
On DeepSeek,

8
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:16,270
the famous Chinese A.I.... eighteen.

9
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:17,600
More than double.

10
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:19,460
Marathi, twenty.

11
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,510
Telugu, twenty-five.

12
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:22,600
And in those two,

13
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,130
it even cuts some of our letters in half.

14
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:29,860
So can India build an A.I. of its own?

15
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:32,180
One that speaks our language best?

16
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:33,520
Here's a clue for you.

17
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:35,030
Ask it in Chinese.

18
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:36,310
Seven pieces.

19
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:37,510
Fewer than English.

20
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:38,970
You see what's going on?

21
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,940
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who built it.

22
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:44,490
And it's not just pride.

23
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,330
Your apps pay these machines by the piece.

24
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,420
More pieces means a bigger bill,

25
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:51,570
and a slower answer for you.

26
00:00:51,770 --> 00:00:53,600
Now think back to last November.

27
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,850
Chat G.P.T.'s paid plan, called Go,

28
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:58,610
became free in India for a whole year.

29
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:00,140
Maybe you signed up too.

30
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,910
OpenAI says India is now one of its biggest markets.

31
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:06,060
For the first people who signed up,

32
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:08,610
that free year runs out... about now.

33
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:09,830
So when yours ends,

34
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:12,210
whose machine will you be talking to?

35
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:13,650
One built in America?

36
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,010
One built in China?

37
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,580
Or one built here?

38
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:19,240
To see why India doesn't have its own yet,

39
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,630
you have to go back to one Monday.

40
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:23,210
The twenty-seventh of January,

41
00:01:23,210 --> 00:01:24,540
twenty twenty-five.

42
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:29,280
A small Chinese lab called DeepSeek has just put out a free A.I.

43
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:30,240
By that Monday,

44
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:34,860
it's beaten Chat G.P.T. in downloads on Apple's App Store in America.

45
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:38,950
DeepSeek says the final training run of its base model

46
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:41,980
cost about five and a half million dollars.

47
00:01:42,090 --> 00:01:44,760
The American giants, you may remember,

48
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,660
were spending billions.

49
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:47,170
That day,

50
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:52,660
the American chip company Nvidia loses around five hundred and ninety billion dollars in value.

51
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:55,350
That's about fifty lakh crore rupees.

52
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:57,550
Think about that number for a second.

53
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:01,440
It's roughly the size of India's whole Union Budget for

54
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,910
that year... gone from one company in one day.

55
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:07,800
Three days later, in Delhi,

56
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,270
India's I.T. minister, Ashwini Vaishnaw,

57
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:11,610
makes a promise.

58
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:17,410
India will build its own A.I. model... and he hopes it will be ready within six months.

59
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,890
So did India keep that promise?

60
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:20,810
You'll see.

61
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:22,160
And around minute ten,

62
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:26,370
I'll run that same chopping test for you again... on an Indian machine.

63
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:27,970
That's where it gets interesting.

64
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:28,630
But first,

65
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:32,200
you need to know what's actually inside one of these machines.

66
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:33,940
Because it isn't magic.

67
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:35,300
It's three things.

68
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:39,230
Picture a child who has read every book in a giant library.

69
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:42,820
You start a sentence... and the child guesses your next word.

70
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:43,950
Then the next one.

71
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:44,920
Then the next.

72
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:47,260
That's really all a chatbot does.

73
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:50,080
It guesses the next piece, again and again,

74
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,770
so well that you feel it's thinking.

75
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:54,410
So if you want to build one,

76
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:55,950
you need three things.

77
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:57,330
A library to read.

78
00:02:57,430 --> 00:02:59,340
A reading room where it can read.

79
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:01,920
And teachers who know how to train the child.

80
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,230
The library is words —

81
00:03:04,230 --> 00:03:06,100
trillions of pieces of text,

82
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:07,980
like the ones you type.

83
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:11,000
The reading room is chips, thousands of them,

84
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,510
running day and night for months.

85
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:14,640
And the teachers?

86
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:15,850
They're engineers.

87
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,470
India has the teachers.

88
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:18,750
You know this already.

89
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:22,470
Indian engineers write software for companies all over the world.

90
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,730
So our missing pieces are the library and the reading room.

91
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,300
Let's start with the library.

92
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:29,750
Almost nobody tells you about this part.

93
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:32,560
A lot of A.I. learns from the internet.

94
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:36,490
One of the biggest free copies of the web is called Common Crawl.

95
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,440
Many A.I. models learned from it,

96
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:42,680
including an early model from the makers of the Chat G.P.T. you use.

97
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:44,400
So we took its latest copy,

98
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,400
from October twenty twenty-six —

99
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,470
more than two billion web pages —

100
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:51,260
and checked its own count of languages.

101
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:52,100
English?

102
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:54,830
Forty-two out of every hundred pages.

103
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:56,490
Now, can you guess Hindi?

104
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:01,280
For every one page in Hindi... there are about two hundred pages in English.

105
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:04,920
And Hindi is the mother tongue of more than fifty crore people.

106
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:05,700
Marathi?

107
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:07,110
About fifteen hundred.

108
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:07,830
Telugu?

109
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:09,720
About two thousand three hundred.

110
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:13,480
And each of those is the mother tongue of around eight crore people,

111
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:14,480
maybe yours.

112
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:19,600
All of India's languages together make up less than one page in every hundred.

113
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:20,520
Think about it.

114
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:24,880
More than one in every six people on Earth lives in our country.

115
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,350
But in the library these machines read,

116
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:29,760
our language is almost invisible.

117
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:33,280
And India's job is harder than China's.

118
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,560
China's A.I. mostly needs one written language.

119
00:04:36,670 --> 00:04:39,440
Our country's Constitution lists twenty-two,

120
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:42,500
written in about a dozen different scripts.

121
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:44,780
Each one needs its own shelf.

122
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:48,650
And that's why your question cost so much more in Hindi,

123
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:50,230
Marathi and Telugu.

124
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:51,560
Remember the chopper?

125
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:54,610
It learns its pieces from this same library.

126
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:56,700
Words it sees all the time,

127
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:58,260
like rain or village,

128
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:00,600
become one neat piece each.

129
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:03,150
Your words, which it hardly ever sees,

130
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:04,920
get chopped into bits.

131
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:07,020
Sometimes into half a letter.

132
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:08,030
Here's the rule.

133
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:10,650
A machine learns from what it reads.

134
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:12,000
And what goes wrong?

135
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:15,630
If our language is only a tiny corner of the library,

136
00:05:15,630 --> 00:05:19,180
the machine learns it last... and charges us more.

137
00:05:19,210 --> 00:05:22,610
So someone has to build the Indian part of the library.

138
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:25,010
Page by page, voice by voice.

139
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:27,480
And that's slow, expensive work.

140
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:29,290
And India has started.

141
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:31,970
A government data shelf for Indian builders,

142
00:05:31,970 --> 00:05:32,950
called A.I. Kosh,

143
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:36,130
now holds more than sixteen thousand collections.

144
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:38,380
But words are only half of it.

145
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:40,040
Even with a perfect library,

146
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:42,020
you need a place to read it.

147
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,620
And this is where India hits a wall.

148
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:46,420
This is the reading room.

149
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,630
A chip like this one,

150
00:05:47,630 --> 00:05:50,270
made by an American company called Nvidia.

151
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:51,720
It's called a G.P.U.,

152
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:54,640
and a single one costs lakhs of rupees.

153
00:05:54,670 --> 00:05:56,110
And to train a big A.I.,

154
00:05:56,110 --> 00:05:58,700
you need thousands of them, wired together,

155
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,140
running for months.

156
00:06:00,170 --> 00:06:01,290
And here's the catch.

157
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,450
India doesn't make these chips.

158
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:06,730
They're designed in America and made mostly in Taiwan.

159
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:09,160
If you watched our chip film, you know why.

160
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:11,700
In January twenty twenty-five,

161
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,730
India found out how fast that door can close.

162
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,640
Just days before leaving office,

163
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,290
America's government put out a new rule.

164
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:21,600
It sorted the world into three groups.

165
00:06:21,630 --> 00:06:24,560
Its closest allies could buy these chips freely.

166
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:26,240
China was blocked.

167
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:27,160
And India?

168
00:06:27,230 --> 00:06:29,100
India was put in the middle group,

169
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,360
with a limit on how many of these chips it could get.

170
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:33,380
Four months later,

171
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,150
the new American government scrapped that rule.

172
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:37,870
But India had seen it.

173
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,330
The tap is in someone else's hand.

174
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:41,930
So what would you do?

175
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:44,820
India didn't wait to own a giant reading room.

176
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:47,150
It rented one... and shared it.

177
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,780
Under the IndiaAI Mission,

178
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:52,390
private companies put their chips into one common pool.

179
00:06:52,420 --> 00:06:54,960
Indian startups and researchers rent time on them,

180
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,390
and the government pays part of the bill.

181
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:59,010
In January twenty twenty-five,

182
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:02,710
India lined up more than eighteen thousand chips for that pool.

183
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:04,330
By June twenty twenty-six,

184
00:07:04,330 --> 00:07:06,470
more than forty-five thousand.

185
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,030
That's a big jump, right?

186
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,310
But by the end of twenty twenty-four,

187
00:07:10,310 --> 00:07:11,920
one American company, Meta,

188
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:16,640
said it would have around three hundred and fifty thousand of these chips.

189
00:07:16,670 --> 00:07:20,840
Almost eight times our country's whole shared pool this June.

190
00:07:20,870 --> 00:07:21,750
And the money?

191
00:07:21,790 --> 00:07:25,400
India's whole A.I. mission is about ten thousand crore rupees,

192
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:27,070
spread over five years.

193
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:28,130
This year alone,

194
00:07:28,130 --> 00:07:31,620
Meta told its investors it would spend around thirteen

195
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:34,470
lakh crore rupees on buildings and machines,

196
00:07:34,470 --> 00:07:36,050
much of it for A.I.

197
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:41,110
So India's whole mission... ten thousand crore,

198
00:07:41,110 --> 00:07:42,440
over five years.

199
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:45,030
Meta alone... thirteen lakh crore,

200
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:46,750
in one single year.

201
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:48,000
That's not double.

202
00:07:48,030 --> 00:07:50,280
That's more than a hundred times as much.

203
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,180
So is it hopeless?

204
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:52,920
No.

205
00:07:52,970 --> 00:07:54,990
Because remember DeepSeek.

206
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:59,130
DeepSeek says it trained its base model on about two thousand chips.

207
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:00,850
Not hundreds of thousands.

208
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:02,640
And they were a cut-down version,

209
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:05,230
made so they could still be sold to China.

210
00:08:05,270 --> 00:08:07,480
So what's the lesson for you and me?

211
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:09,960
You don't always need the biggest room.

212
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:11,850
You need clever engineering.

213
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:14,540
And that's a game Indian engineers can play.

214
00:08:14,570 --> 00:08:16,220
April twenty twenty-five.

215
00:08:16,290 --> 00:08:20,850
The government picks the first company to build India's own model from scratch.

216
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:23,170
A Bengaluru startup called Sarvam.

217
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:25,920
Sarvam was started by two Indians you should know,

218
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:28,100
Vivek Raghavan and Pratyush Kumar.

219
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:32,140
The mission gives them about four thousand of those Nvidia chips to work with.

220
00:08:32,250 --> 00:08:34,840
But a month later, the doubts begin.

221
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:38,490
Sarvam's next model is built on top of a French one.

222
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:41,260
And critics ask — if the base is foreign,

223
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:42,740
how is it Indian?

224
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:44,160
Fair question, isn't it?

225
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:45,360
You'd ask it too.

226
00:08:45,390 --> 00:08:47,920
Can an Indian team train one from zero,

227
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:49,890
start to finish, at home?

228
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,690
February twenty twenty-six.

229
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:53,250
Bharat Mandapam, Delhi.

230
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:55,920
The India A.I. Impact Summit.

231
00:08:55,950 --> 00:08:58,380
There, Sarvam shows India two new models.

232
00:08:58,410 --> 00:09:02,050
The bigger one has a hundred and five billion parameters.

233
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:06,080
Think of them as tiny dials the machine tunes while it reads.

234
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:08,260
Trained from scratch.

235
00:09:08,290 --> 00:09:09,140
In India.

236
00:09:09,250 --> 00:09:11,510
On those shared chips you just saw.

237
00:09:11,670 --> 00:09:13,600
And before it ever talked to you,

238
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:16,640
it read around twelve trillion pieces of text.

239
00:09:16,750 --> 00:09:19,220
Then Sarvam did something generous.

240
00:09:19,250 --> 00:09:20,920
It made the model open.

241
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,800
Any Indian developer can download it and build their own apps on it,

242
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:25,650
for free.

243
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:27,960
The minister had hoped for six months,

244
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:30,120
and later said ten at the most.

245
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:31,600
It took about a year.

246
00:09:31,770 --> 00:09:34,530
Late, you could say... but delivered.

247
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:36,600
And that same week, BharatGen —

248
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,460
a team led by I.I.T. Bombay —

249
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:41,220
showed its own model too, called Param Two,

250
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:44,920
built for all twenty-two scheduled Indian languages.

251
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:48,020
Now remember my promise to you.

252
00:09:48,050 --> 00:09:49,340
The chopping test.

253
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:50,370
Same question.

254
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:52,300
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

255
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:54,330
DeepSeek's chopper, you've seen.

256
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:55,840
Hindi, eighteen.

257
00:09:55,870 --> 00:09:57,300
Marathi, twenty.

258
00:09:57,410 --> 00:09:58,920
Telugu, twenty-five.

259
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,780
The chopper behind Chat G.P.T.'s recent models...

260
00:10:01,910 --> 00:10:05,490
Hindi eight, Marathi fourteen, Telugu fifteen.

261
00:10:05,570 --> 00:10:06,630
And Sarvam's?

262
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:09,750
Hindi, eight — exactly the same as English.

263
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:11,170
Marathi, ten.

264
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:12,570
Telugu, eleven.

265
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:15,750
And this isn't one lucky sentence.

266
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:19,160
We ran the same test on more than a thousand sentences,

267
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:21,420
each one in thirteen languages.

268
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:22,460
Look at Odia.

269
00:10:22,550 --> 00:10:23,720
On DeepSeek's chopper,

270
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:26,390
five and a half times the pieces of English.

271
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:28,890
On Sarvam's... less than one and a half.

272
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,550
And BharatGen's chopper does about as well.

273
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:35,090
So for our language, the gap shrinks, a lot.

274
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:38,810
Because this time... an Indian team put the missing pieces in.

275
00:10:39,530 --> 00:10:41,430
And it's already at work.

276
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:46,180
Sarvam says its models help run Aadhaar services in Indian languages.

277
00:10:46,210 --> 00:10:47,080
And in October,

278
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:52,660
Maharashtra gave Sarvam's A.I. assistant to two thousand five hundred government officials.

279
00:10:52,690 --> 00:10:55,860
So has India built its own Chat G.P.T.?

280
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,500
Let me be honest with you.

281
00:10:57,570 --> 00:11:00,080
Not one that matches the best in the world.

282
00:11:00,110 --> 00:11:00,950
Not yet.

283
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:06,310
DeepSeek's newest big model has one point six trillion of those dials in total.

284
00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:08,370
About fifteen times Sarvam's.

285
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:11,310
And Sarvam says it plainly, in its own report.

286
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:15,400
The newest models from other labs are trained on far bigger clusters,

287
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:17,150
with far bigger budgets.

288
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:18,790
Here's the real scoreboard.

289
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:20,610
India has the teachers.

290
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:22,880
India has started on the library.

291
00:11:22,930 --> 00:11:24,260
And for the reading room...

292
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:26,430
India still rents the chips.

293
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:27,420
What's next?

294
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:32,000
The IndiaAI Mission has now picked twenty Indian proposals to build models,

295
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,200
Sarvam and BharatGen among them.

296
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:36,820
And BharatGen's mandate is to keep scaling,

297
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:39,270
all the way up to a trillion dials.

298
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:40,790
So here's the lesson.

299
00:11:40,850 --> 00:11:44,940
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who build it.

300
00:11:45,010 --> 00:11:47,800
If India only rents other countries' machines,

301
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:52,640
you and I will keep paying extra to be understood... in our own languages.

302
00:11:52,730 --> 00:11:53,450
But today,

303
00:11:53,450 --> 00:11:56,820
there's an Indian machine that reads our Hindi,

304
00:11:56,820 --> 00:11:57,970
Marathi, Telugu,

305
00:11:57,970 --> 00:12:02,770
Bengali or Odia more cheaply than Chat G.P.T. and DeepSeek do.

306
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:04,920
So what does this mean for you?

307
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:05,360
First,

308
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:08,930
check your Chat G.P.T. plan before the free year ends.

309
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:09,780
Once it's over,

310
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:12,150
it can start charging you on its own.

311
00:12:12,910 --> 00:12:14,860
And before you pay for any A.I.,

312
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:19,310
ask the same question in your own language to two or three of them.

313
00:12:19,460 --> 00:12:22,250
Chat G.P.T., DeepSeek, and an Indian one,

314
00:12:22,250 --> 00:12:24,780
like Sarvam's app, called Indus.

315
00:12:25,050 --> 00:12:28,230
Pay only for the one that really understands you.

316
00:12:28,450 --> 00:12:32,120
And A.I. isn't the only thing India has built for itself.

317
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:35,680
Did you know India has its own satellite navigation,

318
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:36,790
up in space?

319
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:38,430
It's called NavIC.

320
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:42,530
So why does your phone still lean on America's G.P.S.?

321
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:44,210
That's our next story.

322
00:12:44,390 --> 00:12:46,610
Before you go, try this tonight.

323
00:12:46,670 --> 00:12:49,910
Ask any A.I. one question in your mother tongue... and

324
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:53,140
tell me in the comments which one answered best.

325
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:54,640
I read every one.

326
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:56,960
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

327
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:58,090
Stay practical.

328
00:12:58,130 --> 00:12:58,850
Jai Hind.
